<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Komaki and Nagakute</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1584">1584</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Komaki-Nagakute campaign</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Komaki and Nagakute was a campaign fought in 1584 in Owari province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and the allied forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a>. Ieyasu won a notable victory at Nagakute, where Hideyoshi's commanders Ikeda Tsuneoki and Mori Nagayoshi were killed, but the campaign ended in stalemate. Ieyasu then made peace and became Hideyoshi's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-komaki-and-nagakute.jpg" alt="Battle of Komaki and Nagakute" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 立花左近 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagakute_Historic_Battlefield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1583 Hashiba Hideyoshi, who would take the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> in 1586, backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobukatsu">Oda Nobukatsu</a>, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Battle of Shizugatake</a>. When Hideyoshi then invited Nobukatsu and other generals to pay homage at his new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>, Nobukatsu refused. Rumours that his chief retainers Tsugawa Yoshifuyu, Okada Shigetaka and Azai Nagatoki supported Hideyoshi led Nobukatsu to have them executed on 17 March 1584. This gave Hideyoshi his reason to attack, and Nobukatsu called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, so that the war became one between Hideyoshi and Ieyasu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At Haguro, Mori Nagayoshi, eager to match the achievements of his father-in-law Ikeda Tsuneoki, advanced beyond Inuyama Castle and was surprised by Sakai Tadatsugu, retreating with 300 casualties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> fortified Mount Komaki and Hideyoshi built forts of his own, and the two armies faced each other in a stalemate. Ikeda Tsuneoki then set out with an army numbering 20,000 to raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a>. His forces took Iwasaki Castle, where Niwa Ujitsugu lost 300 killed, among them his brother Niwa Ujishige.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s troops surprised and wiped out the force of Miyoshi Hidetsugu resting in Hakusan Forest, and Hori Hidemasa, who came to its aid, was forced back when the main Tokugawa army of some 9,000 arrived. In the final battle at Nagakute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>'s musketeers repeatedly broke the charges of Tsuneoki and Nagayoshi for over two hours. Nagayoshi was shot dead, Tsuneoki was killed by the spear of Nagai Naokatsu, and Tsuneoki's son Ikeda Motosuke also fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hideyoshi withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, and the war moved to sieges such as that of Kanie Castle. Nobukatsu made a separate peace with Hideyoshi in November 1584 and became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> no longer had a clear justification for continuing the war. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the Tokugawa records called the fighting the Battle of Komaki. The Nagakute Battlefield Historic Site was designated a National Historic Site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Komaki and Nagakute” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He fought Toyotomi Hideyoshi to a draw at <b>Komaki and Nagakute</b> in 1584, then became his leading vassal, administering through loyal hereditary retainers.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
